  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "The rate $O$ of the orders a car factory receives can be modeled as $O(t)=100t{e}^{-0.1t}$ cars per day. The rate at which cars are completed can be modeled as $C(t)=50+10sin\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}t)$ cars per day. In both cases, $t$ is the time, in days, since the launch of the new model. \n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction $28$ days after the model's launch? Assume that an ordered car goes into construction as soon as the factory receives its order. Round your answer to the nearest integer.",
  "worked_solution": "The rate of change of the number of cars under construction at time $t$ is equal to the order rate minus the completion rate, that is,\n\n$$\nO(t)-C(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=28,$ the rate of change in the number of cars under construction after $28$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(t)-C(t) & =100(28){e}^{-0.1(28)}-(50+10sin\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}(28))) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50-10sin\u2061(8\\pi ) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50 \\\\  & \u2248120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of cars in construction after $28$ days is $120$ cars per day.",
